tel 1.0

base vexillology
import soundness

info   sets of an enumeration's cases packed into the bits of one `Long`, tested and set by case
detail
    _Vexillology_ gives bit flags a typed surface. `Flags[Color]` is a set of on-or-off
    flags over the cases of the enumeration `Color`, held in the bits of a single `Long`,
    each case owning the bit of its ordinal. A flag is tested and set by naming the case
    rather than by a magic bit number, so a bit which means something else, or nothing,
    cannot be reached; and the value stays one machine word — free to copy, cheap to store,
    natural in a binary format — where a `Set[Color]` would allocate.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    enum Color:
      case Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet

    import Color.*

    var flags = Flags[Color]()
    flags = flags(Orange) = true
    flags = flags(Green) = true

    flags(Orange)   // true
    flags(Red)      // false
    flags.set       // Set(Orange, Green)
    ```

    A `Flags` value is immutable: each assignment makes a new one, so the variable is
    reassigned with the update syntax, `flags = flags(Orange) = true`. The operations need
    the enumeration to be `Enumerable`, which every Scala 3 `enum` of singleton cases is.

    The library has a single source set, `vexillology.core`, and everything is exported to
    the `soundness` umbrella.

refer vexillology.Flags

entry .Flags   a set of an enumeration's cases, packed as bits into one `Long`
  detail
      `Flags[Enumeration]` is the opaque type `vexillology.internal.Flags`, exported here: a
      `Long` in which bit *n* is set when the enumeration's case of ordinal *n* is in the
      set. `Flags[Color]()` is the empty set, and three extension methods, in implicit scope
      without any import, use it:

      - `flags(case)` tests whether a case is set;
      - `flags(case) = enabled` (that is, `flags.update(case, enabled)`) makes a new value
        with the case set or cleared;
      - `flags.set` reads the flags out as an ordinary `Set` of the cases.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Permission:
        case Read, Write, Execute

      val none = Flags[Permission]()
      val readable = none(Permission.Read) = true
      val both = readable(Permission.Write) = true

      both(Permission.Write)   // true
      both.set.size            // 2
      ```

      A `Long` has 64 bits, so an enumeration of up to 64 cases fits. Beyond that, a case's
      bit position wraps around and it shares a bit with a lower case: there is no check.
      Each method takes a case of a singleton type, so an enum case with parameters cannot
      be a flag.
  refer vexillology.internal

entry .internal   the home of the opaque `Flags` type and its operations
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists to hold the opaque type `Flags` and the
      extension methods on it, which are in implicit scope wherever a `Flags` is, since they
      share its enclosing object. `Flags` is exported from here to the `vexillology`
      package, whose page has the full account.
  entry .Flags   a set of an enumeration's cases packed into a `Long`; see `vexillology.Flags`
    detail
        The opaque type over `Long`. Its page under the `vexillology` package has the full
        account.
    entry .apply   the empty set of flags over an enumeration, with no bit set, `Flags[Color]()`
  entry .apply   whether a case is set in some flags, `flags(case)`
      Needs the enumeration to be `Enumerable`, to find the case's ordinal.
  entry .update   a copy of some flags with a case set or cleared, `flags(case) = enabled`
      Returns the new `Flags` rather than changing the receiver, so a variable holding the
      flags is reassigned: `flags = flags(Orange) = true`.
  entry .set   the cases set in some flags, as a `Set` of the enumeration
      Visits only the set bits, lowest first.
